  "subject": "😮‍💨 Axios Finish Line: Stress relief trend",
  "date": "Mon, 19 Oct 2026 21:29:31 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 💫 Plus: Starstruck | Monday, October 19, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PRESENTED BY PEPSICO \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 19, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 391 words … 1½ mins. Edited by Natalie Daher and copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Your face needs a break \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n An esthetician massages a client's face. Photo: Hyoung Chang/The Denver Post via Getty Images One spot getting more massage treatment: The face.\n\n The big picture: Facial massage is gaining popularity as people seek relief from tension and a new way to relax, Axios' Carly Mallenbaum reports. \n\n \"We're holding more tension in the face,\" thanks partly to the strain of looking at screens, Twin Cities esthetician Christina Snead tells Axios. Think forehead, jaw and chin. 📈 Zoom in: Demand for facial massage has grown at Snead's Berkana studio in St. Louis Park, Minnesota.\n\n Four years ago, she performed roughly two or three a week. Now, she may do as many as 20, she says. FaceGym, a facial massage chain founded in London, has two more U.S. locations planned , adding to its seven already open in New York and Los Angeles. 😮‍💨 How it works: Facial massage takes many forms, including gua sha , a technique rooted in centuries-old Chinese medicine that uses a smooth-edged tool to massage or scrape the skin.\n\n Snead starts with warm gua sha stones, then uses her hands to massage facial muscles before finishing with cool stones. Clients often say their faces feel \"lighter\" and less puffy afterward. Some even cry, which Snead describes as an emotional release. What they're saying: Facial massage \"would conceivably have the same physiological effects as massaging other parts of the body,\" says Tiffany Field, a faculty member at the University of Miami School of Medicine who studies massage.\n\n 💤 Reality check: Research on facial massage is limited, though small studies suggest it may help people sleep better . Studies of facial rollers also suggest they may boost blood flow and improve skin elasticity .\n\n FaceGym promotes cosmetic benefits, including lifting, sculpting and toning facial muscles, but the evidence for lasting cosmetic improvements remains limited. 😌 Try it yourself: Snead recommends gently rolling a jade roller from the nose toward the ear and then down the neck.\n\n \"It doesn't have to be a long time,\" she says. The bottom line: For many clients, the appeal is simple: \"A rest, a little breath, a little peace for a second,\" Snead says.\n\n Share this story . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM PEPSICO\r\n\n \r\n How prioritizing leadership boosts transformation \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PepsiCo combines AI and data with people-first leadership to help teams move faster and make smarter decisions with real results.\n\n\r\n The impact: Empowered associates can use shared insights to act with more confidence and turn transformation into everyday business value.\n\n\r\n Explore PepsiCo’s strategy. \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🌌 Parting shot: Cosmic coast \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Shane Huynh Axios San Diego reader Shane Huynh, a local photographer, recently snapped this beautiful photo of the Milky Way over the Mission Beach jetty.\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
